/-!
# Appendix: logic

The imported named-syntax modules prove assignment locality, termination of
capture-avoiding substitution, its exact free-variable formula and semantic
substitution lemma. Alpha-equivalence generated by fresh binder renaming
preserves free variables and truth; substitution for an absent variable gives
an alpha-equivalent formula. Comparison under corresponding variable names
characterizes alpha-equivalence and proves that substitution descends to
alpha-classes, including instantiation with different bound names.
Translation into mathlib's canonical formulas preserves interpretation.
The displayed natural-deduction rules are sound, including the existential
eigenvariable condition; a model implies consistency. Simultaneous variable
permutations and weakening are admissible, proved together by induction with
fresh eigenvariables. Finite-support deduction and consistency lemmas give the
Lindenbaum maximal consistent extension by Zorn, with deductive closure and
deciding every sentence. Language maps preserve substitution and derivations;
new Henkin constants give closed witness axioms. Representatives with fresh
bound names make constant elimination well-defined on alpha-classes and
compatible with substitution. Constant elimination proves conservativity of
constant extensions and consistency of one fresh Henkin axiom. Moving one
selected constant to a further fresh slot proves consistency for finite
families; finite proof support extends this to an arbitrary witness family
from one language stage. Successive languages carry consistent theories;
retractions into positive stages prove consistency inside the final language.
Every final formula comes from a finite stage, giving a consistent extension
with all Henkin witnesses. Equality elimination proves equivalence and congruence
of provable equality on closed terms, giving a nonempty quotient structure.
Term evaluation gives the atomic truth clauses. Maximal-theory membership rules
and Henkin witnesses give sentence truth by strong induction on formula size;
successive closed substitutions give the assignment truth lemma. The term model
and its reduct yield completeness, then finite proof support yields compactness.
Formula induction proves Tarski--Vaught, and the explicit finite-stage closure
under original and Skolem functions gives the countable downward theorem.
Cardinal estimates on the stages give the general Skolem bound. Counting the
actual named syntax, Henkin requests, tagged constants and closed-term classes
also bounds the completeness construction itself. Model expansion gives a shared
conservativity criterion; explicit relation definitions give a unique expansion
and instantiate that criterion. Unique graph values similarly give the unique
expansion by function and constant definitions, and substitution proves the
single-witness elimination equivalence. Two substitution passes through fresh
names implement simultaneous substitution, with its semantic and free-variable
bounds. Recursive elimination of relation symbols preserves truth and free-variable
bounds on alpha-classes: fresh representatives reduce simultaneous substitution
to literal replacement, and comparison under variable-name relations proves
independence of bound-name choices. Literal composition proves compatibility
with substitution, and induction on natural-deduction rules preserves derivations
and eigenvariable conditions. Translated relation axioms have pure logical proofs;
replacing these assumptions gives syntactic conservativity. Recursive old-language
term graphs bind the values of all children and preserve their original parameters.
They give truth-preserving elimination of nested function terms without new free
variables. Pairing witness blocks proves syntactic correspondence of recursive
graphs, independence of fresh starting indices and compatibility with bound-name
changes. Function elimination therefore descends to alpha-classes. Opening the
unique-existence abbreviation gives a universal graph characterization; existential
and equality rules then derive both implications of graph-witness replacement.
Independent finite witnesses can be opened and bound together. Unique-existence
axioms give operation values at fresh inputs by permutation of names; induction
then derives existence of every recursive term value and every finite tuple of
argument values in the old theory. Equality elimination in fresh argument holes
transports simultaneous instances. Operation uniqueness, disjoint child blocks,
and induction then prove pairwise equality of recursive values. Substitution
changes only the output name; combining functionality with existence proves the
full unique-existence abbreviation. Functional name correspondences prove that
variable substitution commutes with elimination even when names merge. One
term value supplies both witnesses in a syntactic proof of translated reflexivity.
Recursive graphs compose syntactically under arbitrary term substitution: one
induction gives the forward direction, and existence plus functionality gives
the reverse. Binding the replacement value yields the relational equivalence.
Pointwise graph implications lift through a finite witness block. A shared
value-binding lemma then proves relational substitution for atomic relations,
equalities and falsity, with the canonical witness choices matched by alpha
comparison. Conditional equivalences extend through every connective and both
quantifiers on representatives with fresh binders. Binding the replacement value
once at the end proves relational substitution for arbitrary formulas.
Graph values implement universal instantiation and existential introduction.
Opening the two graphs in a translated equality gives the ordinary equality
step between values. Induction then translates every natural-deduction rule
into the old sentence theory together with the translated assumptions; finite
proof support extends the result to arbitrary sets of assumptions.
Bounded unfolding composes Delta-zero expansions and binds function values
inside already available bounds. Its correctness uses bounded unique existence;
subset and binary-union examples reuse or expose their bounded formulas.
